ctlsock: an absolute pointer needs no counts — state it in the guest's own RAM

Applies AFTER mame-ctlsock-open-loop-gain.patch (same file, later hunks).

THE WALL. A quadrature-mouse machine gives the module nothing to read, so
MOVEA degrades to open-loop dead reckoning: issue Delta-px/gain counts and
believe you arrived. That can never be 1:1, for two reasons that no amount of
gain tuning removes.

  1. The gain is not a constant. The Macintosh ROM's mouse task applies its
     own ACCELERATION curve at VBL, so one count is worth ~1 guest px when the
     counts trickle and ~2 when they arrive in a burst. MEASURED on macsys1
     (mac128k, golden.sta, MAME_CTL_PTR_MOD=256): MOVEP 30 20 from RawMouse
     (15,15) landed RawMouse at (70,53) — 1.83 px/count across, 1.90 down,
     while the wave's own bulk-travel measurement read 1.98. One number
     cannot describe both.
  2. A LETTERBOX puts the guest's origin somewhere other than the surface's.
     mac128k's 512x342 raster is drawn 2x inside the published 1024x768
     surface as 1024x684 with a 42 px band top and bottom, so the guest's
     (0,0) is published (0,42) — and an open loop that homes by slamming into
     its own belief's corner lands every target 42 px low, with the bottom
     84 px of the coordinate space unreachable. macsys1 shipped
     stream.pointer.transport=none for exactly this.

THE FIX. The guest OS keeps the pointer in RAM, and RAM is addressable from
the module. State the visitor's pixel there and nudge the OS to republish it:
no counts are issued, so neither acceleration nor accumulator drift can exist,
and the letterbox is one explicit rectangle instead of a homing ritual.

On a Macintosh the globals are the documented low-memory ones — MTemp $828,
RawMouse $82C, Mouse $830 (Point is {v,h}, big-endian) — plus the documented
republish idiom CrsrNew $8CE := CrsrCouple $8CF, which is how the Toolbox is
told the position changed outside the mouse interrupt. Nothing here is
Mac-specific in the CODE: the addresses, the Point order and the flag are all
env, so any guest whose pointer lives in RAM binds the same way.

  MAME_CTL_ABS_RAM    cpu=<tag>,pts=<addr>+<addr>...,order=vh|hv,
                      flag=<addr>,flagsrc=<addr>   (unset => nothing changes)
  MAME_CTL_ABS_RECT   x,y,w,h — the published rect the raster occupies
                      (the letterbox; default = the whole surface)
  MAME_CTL_ABS_GEOM   WxH — the raster's size in GUEST px (default = the rect)

MOVEA then short-circuits the whole dead-reckoning engine before it can issue
a single count, and keeps the belief in step so a fallback (an unresolvable
address space) is not left believing an origin it never reached.

Three diagnostic verbs come with it — PEEK/POKEW/POKEB on the same space —
because an absolute binding must be DERIVED, not guessed: move the pointer
with MOVEP, read the candidate globals, keep the address that tracked. That
is exactly how the three Mac addresses above were confirmed, on the rig, in
one pass.

MEASURED on the macsys1 rig (golden.sta, the fleet binary + this patch),
MAME_CTL_ABS_RECT=0,42,1024,684 MAME_CTL_ABS_GEOM=512x342:

  five targets x two laps, exact-match sprite readback on the published
  1024x768 surface — (26,68) (998,68) (26,694) (998,694) (512,384) —
  WORST |err| = 0 px, both laps identical.
  RawMouse readback at the same five: exactly the commanded guest pixel,
  (+0,+0) on every one.

Every default is unset, so a station that does not set the knobs builds and
behaves exactly as before.

